# 1. Array Methods

An **array** is a data structure used to store multiple values in a single variable.

```javascript
const fruits = ["Apple", "Mango", "Orange"];

console.log(fruits);
```

Output:

```text
["Apple", "Mango", "Orange"]
```

JavaScript provides many built-in methods to work with arrays.

### 🔹 Common Array Methods

| Method | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `push()` | Add item to the end |
| `pop()` | Remove item from the end |
| `shift()` | Remove first item |
| `unshift()` | Add item to the beginning |
| `slice()` | Create a portion of an array |
| `splice()` | Add/remove items |
| `includes()` | Check whether a value exists |
| `indexOf()` | Find the index of a value |
| `forEach()` | Execute code for each item |
| `map()` | Transform every item |
| `filter()` | Select matching items |
| `find()` | Find the first matching item |
| `reduce()` | Reduce values to one result |

---

## 🔹 `push()`

Adds an element to the end of an array.

```javascript
const fruits = ["Apple", "Mango"];

fruits.push("Orange");

console.log(fruits);
```

Output:

```text
["Apple", "Mango", "Orange"]
```

---

## 🔹 `pop()`

Removes the last element.

```javascript
const fruits = ["Apple", "Mango", "Orange"];

fruits.pop();

console.log(fruits);
```

Output:

```text
["Apple", "Mango"]
```

---

## 🔹 `includes()`

Checks whether an array contains a particular value.

```javascript
const languages = ["JavaScript", "Python", "C"];

console.log(languages.includes("Python"));
```

Output:

```text
true
```

---

## 🔹 `forEach()`

Runs a function for every element.

```javascript
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];

numbers.forEach(number => {
    console.log(number);
});
```

Output:

```text
1
2
3
4
```

> ⚠️ `forEach()` does not create a new transformed array. For transformations, `map()` is usually more appropriate.

---

# 2. `map()`

The `map()` method creates a **new array** by applying a function to every element.

### Syntax

```javascript
array.map(callback);
```

---

## 🔹 Basic Example

```javascript
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];

const doubled = numbers.map(number => number * 2);

console.log(doubled);
```

Output:

```text
[2, 4, 6, 8]
```

The original array remains:

```javascript
console.log(numbers);
```

Output:

```text
[1, 2, 3, 4]
```

---

## 🔹 Transforming Strings

```javascript
const names = ["abhinav", "arun", "rahul"];

const upperNames = names.map(name => name.toUpperCase());

console.log(upperNames);
```

Output:

```text
["ABHINAV", "ARUN", "RAHUL"]
```

---

## 🔹 Mapping Objects

Consider:

```javascript
const users = [
    { name: "Abhinav", age: 20 },
    { name: "Arun", age: 21 },
    { name: "Rahul", age: 22 }
];
```

Extract only the names:

```javascript
const names = users.map(user => user.name);

console.log(names);
```

Output:

```text
["Abhinav", "Arun", "Rahul"]
```

### 🧠 Remember

```text
map()
 ↓
Every item
 ↓
Transform
 ↓
New array
```

