# 6. Rendering Lists Dynamically

One of the most common uses of arrays in React is **rendering lists dynamically**.

Instead of manually writing every element:

```jsx
<h2>JavaScript</h2>
<h2>React</h2>
<h2>Django</h2>
```

We can store the data in an array:

```javascript
const skills = ["JavaScript", "React", "Django"];
```

Then use `map()` to render the list.

---

## 🔹 Basic React Example

```jsx
function Skills() {
    const skills = ["JavaScript", "React", "Django"];

    return (
        <div>
            {skills.map(skill => (
                <h2>{skill}</h2>
            ))}
        </div>
    );
}

export default Skills;
```

React generates:

```text
JavaScript
React
Django
```

---

## 🔑 The `key` Prop

When rendering lists in React, each item should have a unique `key`.

```jsx
const skills = ["JavaScript", "React", "Django"];

function Skills() {
    return (
        <div>
            {skills.map((skill, index) => (
                <h2 key={index}>{skill}</h2>
            ))}
        </div>
    );
}
```

For simple static examples, an index can work. However, when your data has a unique ID, use that ID instead.

---

## 🔹 Rendering Objects

Consider:

```javascript
const users = [
    {
        id: 1,
        name: "Abhinav",
        role: "Developer"
    },
    {
        id: 2,
        name: "Arun",
        role: "Designer"
    },
    {
        id: 3,
        name: "Rahul",
        role: "Tester"
    }
];
```

We can render them dynamically:

```jsx
function Users() {
    return (
        <div>
            {users.map(user => (
                <div key={user.id}>
                    <h2>{user.name}</h2>
                    <p>{user.role}</p>
                </div>
            ))}
        </div>
    );
}
```

