# 🔥 Combining `filter()` and `map()`

Array methods can be chained together.

For example, display only adult users:

```javascript
const users = [
    { id: 1, name: "Abhinav", age: 20 },
    { id: 2, name: "Arun", age: 16 },
    { id: 3, name: "Rahul", age: 25 }
];
```

```jsx
function Adults() {
    return (
        <div>
            {users
                .filter(user => user.age >= 18)
                .map(user => (
                    <p key={user.id}>
                        {user.name}
                    </p>
                ))}
        </div>
    );
}
```

### Data Flow

```text
users
  ↓
filter()
  ↓
Adults only
  ↓
map()
  ↓
React elements
  ↓
UI
```

---

# 🔥 Combining `find()`

Suppose we need a particular user:

```javascript
const user = users.find(user => user.id === 2);
```

Now:

```jsx
<p>{user?.name}</p>
```

The optional chaining operator `?.` prevents an error if `user` is `undefined`.

