# 🚀 Data Transformation Flow

Modern React applications frequently follow this pattern:

```text
       ARRAY DATA
           │
           ▼
      filter()
           │
           ▼
   Select required data
           │
           ▼
       map()
           │
           ▼
   Transform into UI
           │
           ▼
      React renders
```

For calculations:

```text
       ARRAY DATA
           │
           ▼
       reduce()
           │
           ▼
    Single result
           │
           ▼
      Display UI
```

For searching:

```text
       ARRAY DATA
           │
           ▼
        find()
           │
           ▼
   First matching item
```

---

# ⚡ `map()` vs `filter()` vs `find()` vs `reduce()`

| Method | Returns | Main Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `map()` | New array | Transform every item |
| `filter()` | New array | Select matching items |
| `find()` | Single item / `undefined` | Find first match |
| `reduce()` | Single value | Accumulate into one result |

### Easy Memory Trick

```text
MAP    → Transform 🔄
FILTER → Select 🔍
FIND   → Search 🎯
REDUCE → Combine ➕
```

---

# ⚛️ React Example: Complete Data Transformation

```jsx
const products = [
    { id: 1, name: "Laptop", price: 60000, category: "Electronics" },
    { id: 2, name: "Mouse", price: 1000, category: "Electronics" },
    { id: 3, name: "Book", price: 500, category: "Education" },
    { id: 4, name: "Keyboard", price: 2000, category: "Electronics" }
];

function Products() {
    const electronics = products.filter(
        product => product.category === "Electronics"
    );

    return (
        <div>
            {electronics.map(product => (
                <div key={product.id}>
                    <h2>{product.name}</h2>
                    <p>₹{product.price}</p>
                </div>
            ))}
        </div>
    );
}

export default Products;
```

### What happens?

```text
products
   ↓
filter()
   ↓
Electronics products
   ↓
map()
   ↓
React elements
   ↓
Rendered UI
```

